// lookups walk the collision chain`,label:"Under heavy collisions"},{code:"// the map grows past a million entries",label:"When it gets large"},{code:"// keys are negative numbers",label:"With negative keys"},{code:"// values are null",label:"With null values"}],correct:0,explanation:"O(1) assumes keys spread evenly across buckets. When many keys collide — bad hashCode, or adversarial inputs — lookups walk a chain, degrading toward O(n). Java 8 caps the damage by turning long chains into red-black trees, making the worst case O(log n). Size alone does not hurt (that is what amortized O(1) means), and negative keys or null values are perfectly legal.",hint:'The guarantee says "average". What assumption about the keys does the average rest on?'}],25:[{type:"trace",prompt:"Push 1, 2, 3, pop twice — what does peek see?",code:[{text:"Deque<Integer> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();"},{text:"stack.push(1); stack.push(2); stack.push(3);"},{text:"stack.pop(); stack.pop();"},{text:"System.out.println(stack.peek());",highlight:!0}],options:["1","2","3","null"],correct:0,explanation:"push adds at the head of the deque, so the order inside is 3,2,1 — the pops remove 3 then 2, and peek reads (without removing) the remaining 1. null is what peek returns on an EMPTY ArrayDeque, one more pop away. Note pop() on empty throws instead; the peek/poll family returns null — a deliberate API split worth knowing.",hint:"Two questions: which element survives both pops, and does peek remove it?"},{type:"pair",prompt:"Match each class to its role",pairs:[{id:1,left:"ArrayDeque",right:"The stack/deque to reach for"},{id:2,left:"Stack class",right:"Legacy, extends Vector"},{id:3,left:"LinkedList",right:"Deque with linked storage"},{id:4,left:"PriorityQueue",right:"Heap — best out first"}],explanation:"The JDK's own Javadoc for Stack says to use Deque instead: Stack extends Vector, so every operation takes a lock nobody asked for, and its iterator walks bottom-to-top — the reverse of what a stack means. PriorityQueue is not FIFO at all: poll() returns the smallest element, regardless of arrival."},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Assemble the balanced-brackets checker",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"boolean isValid(String s) {"},{id:"b",text:"    Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();"},{id:"c",text:"    Map<Character,Character> pairs = Map.of(')','(', ']','[', '}','{');"},{id:"d",text:"    for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {"},{id:"e",text:'        if ("([{".indexOf(c) >= 0) stack.push(c);'},{id:"f",text:"        else if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(c))"},{id:"g",text:"            return false;"},{id:"h",text:"    }"},{id:"i",text:"    return stack.isEmpty();"},{id:"j",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"k",text:"    return true;"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h","i","j"],explanation:'isEmpty must be checked before popping — a closer with nothing open would otherwise throw NoSuchElementException. The final line must verify emptiness: plain return true passes "(((". One Java-specific footnote: stack.pop() != pairs.get(c) compares a char against an unboxed Character here; with boxed Characters on both sides, != would compare references and lie.',hint:"Three ways to be unbalanced: wrong match, closer-first, opener never closed. Find the line that catches each."},{type:"bug",prompt:"Code review rejects this line. Why?",code:[{text:"Stack<Integer> s = new Stack<>();",hasError:!0},{text:"s.push(1); s.push(2);",hasError:!1},{text:"for (int x : s) System.out.println(x);",hasError:!1}],correctLine:0,explanation:"Stack extends Vector, a Java 1.0 relic: every method is synchronized (a cost single-threaded code pays for nothing), and — the trap in line 3 — its for-each iterates BOTTOM-to-top, printing 1 then 2, the opposite of stack order. Deque<Integer> s = new ArrayDeque<>() fixes both: faster, and its iterator goes top-down as a stack should read.",hint:"Run line 3 mentally. Which element prints first, and is that the order a stack should show?"}],26:[{type:"trace",prompt:"What is a.next.val in this chain?",code:[{text:"class Node { int val; Node next; }"},{text:"Node a=new Node(1), b=new Node(2), c=new Node(3);"},{text:"a.next=b; b.next=c;"},{text:"System.out.println(a.next.val);",highlight:!0}],options:["1","2","3","NPE"],correct:1,explanation:"a.next points at b, and b.val is 2. Reading a chain of dots left to right — a, then .next (b), then .val — is the core skill for every linked-structure problem. The NPE option becomes real one link further: c.next is null, so c.next.val throws. Every dot after a possibly-null reference is a hazard.",hint:"Resolve one dot at a time: what object is a.next? Then take its val."},{type:"scramble",prompt:"Assemble the in-place list reversal",pieces:[{id:"a",text:"Node reverse(Node head) {"},{id:"b",text:"    Node prev = null, curr = head;"},{id:"c",text:"    while (curr != null) {"},{id:"d",text:"        Node next = curr.next;"},{id:"e",text:"        curr.next = prev;"},{id:"f",text:"        prev = curr;"},{id:"g",text:"        curr = next;"},{id:"h",text:"    }"},{id:"i",text:"    return prev;"},{id:"j",text:"}"}],distractors:[{id:"k",text:"        curr.next = next;"}],correctOrder:["a","b","c","d","e","f","g","h","i","j"],explanation:"The save (Node next = curr.next) must come before the overwrite (curr.next = prev) — reversed, the rest of the list is unreachable. When the loop ends curr is null, so the new head is prev, not curr: returning curr returns null and the reversal vanishes. The spare line undoes the flip it follows.",hint:"Why is next saved first? And when the loop exits, which variable is holding the last real node?"},{type:"realworld",prompt:"General-purpose list in Java — which default, and why?",scenario:"🤔 Both implement List. The costs differ underneath.",options:[{code:"new ArrayList<>()  // contiguous array",label:"ArrayList"},{code:"new LinkedList<>() // node chain",label:"LinkedList"},{code:"new Vector<>()     // synchronized array",label:"Vector"},{code:"new int[16]        // raw array",label:"A raw array"}],correct:0,explanation:"ArrayList wins almost always, and the deep reason is hardware: contiguous memory means cache hits, while every LinkedList node is a separate allocation with a pointer hop — even its theoretical O(1) head-insert often loses in practice. LinkedList earns its keep only for heavy add/remove at both ends, and even there ArrayDeque is usually better. Vector is the legacy synchronized relic, and a raw array cannot grow.",hint:"Think about where the elements live in memory, and what the CPU cache thinks of that."},{type:"bug",prompt:"This crashes on an empty list. Which line?",code:[{text:"Node curr = head;",hasError:!1},{text:"while (curr.next != null) {",hasError:!0},{text:"    curr = curr.next;",hasError:!1},{text:"}",hasError:!1}],correctLine:1,explanation:"If head is null, curr.next dereferences null on the very first check — NullPointerException. The condition must be curr != null, which also fixes a second, quieter bug: curr.next != null stops AT the last node instead of after it, so the final element never gets processed. One wrong condition, two distinct failures.",hint:"Feed an empty list (head = null) into line 2. What does curr.next try to do?"}],27:[{type:"trace",prompt:"In-order traversal of this BST prints what?",code:[{text:"//       4"},{text:"//      / \\"},{text:"//     2   6"},{text:"//    / \\"},{text:"//   1   3"},{text:"// in-order: visit left subtree, node, right subtree",highlight:!0}],options:["4 2 1 3 6","1 2 3 4 6","1 3 2 6 4","4 2 6 1 3"],correct:1,explanation:"In-order goes fully left first: 1, back to 2, then 3, up to 4, then 6 — ascending order. That is not luck: BST invariant plus in-order = sorted output, always. This is literally what TreeMap does when you iterate it — its keys arrive sorted because the iterator is an in-order walk of a red-black tree.",hint:"Fully left before every node, right after. Start at the deepest left leaf."},{type:"pair",prompt:"Match each class to what is underneath",pairs:[{id:1,left:"TreeMap",right:"Red-black BST"},{id:2,left:"PriorityQueue",right:"Binary heap"},{id:3,left:"TreeSet",right:"TreeMap with values ignored"},{id:4,left:"HashMap",right:"Hash table — no tree"}],explanation:"The implementation dictates the contract: TreeMap iterates in sorted key order and offers firstKey/subMap because a red-black tree can, PriorityQueue only ever promises the TOP element (iterating it is NOT sorted — a heap is not a sorted structure), and HashMap promises no order at all. TreeSet is literally a TreeMap wearing a Set interface.",hint:"Ask what each one can promise about ORDER. The underlying structure decides."},{type:"fillgap",prompt:"val is SMALLER than this node — which way does the search go?",codeParts:[{text:`Node search(Node root, int val) {
